-- title:
--   The clip map is $1$-Lipschitz
-- statement:
--   The clipping map is $1$-Lipschitz in its first argument: for all reals $t, s, lo, hi$,
--   $$\bigl|\mathrm{clip}(t, lo, hi) - \mathrm{clip}(s, lo, hi)\bigr| \;\le\; |t - s| , \qquad \mathrm{clip}(t, lo, hi) \;=\; \max\{lo,\ \min\{hi, t\}\}.$$
--   Here $t$ and $s$ are the two arguments being compared, $lo$ and $hi$ are the fixed clipping bounds, and $\mathrm{clip}$ is the clamp that appears in the gradient (26.3) as $\mathrm{clip}(\cdot, -c, c)$. The inequality is one of the two $1$-Lipschitz inputs to the gradient bound of the lifted Huber function.
